## Code Formatting

Companion to `foundation-engineering.mdc`. Readability comes from consistency, not compactness.

Write the code consistently first. Prettier only normalizes width, quotes, semicolons, and commas afterward. Do not lean on Prettier to make inconsistent code look uniform, and do not hand-wrap what Prettier will re-wrap. Compact to a single line only when the statement is truly singular.

Layers own different things:

- Author: how `const`, `if`, `return`, guards, blank lines, grouping, object literals, signatures, and interface order are written.
- ESLint pins structure Prettier ignores: brace style (`curly: multi-line`), value-vs-type imports (`consistent-type-imports`), and member order in interfaces and classes (`member-ordering`), which covers required before optional, fields before methods, and public before private.
- Prettier normalizes only: `printWidth: 100`, import grouping and type-import separation, quotes, semicolons, commas.

### Statements

- One declaration per `const`. Do not pad every `const` with blank lines.
- Use a single-line guard only when the whole line fits within `printWidth: 100`, such as `if (!x) return`. Once the condition or body wraps, use braces on their own lines.
- Brace the guard from the start when the body is a long expression Prettier will wrap, such as a template literal or a call. A bare one-line `if (cond) return` whose body Prettier pushes onto the next line fails `curly: multi-line`, so never author `if (cond) return \`...long...\`` on one line.
- Keep the same guard and return shape across a function. Do not mix one-line and braced forms for equivalent cases.

### Object literals

- Author a multi-field object multi-line, one field per line, and keep it that way. Prettier preserves the break when the first field starts on its own line after `{`.
- Use a single line only for a single-field or trivial object.
- Do not nest a one-line multi-field object inside a multi-line one.
- In a large literal, group fields into logical clusters separated by a single blank line.

### Signatures

- Do not inline a multi-field object type into a parameter or return position. Name it as an `interface` or `type` and reference the name, so the signature shows intent.
- A single-field inline type may stay inline.

### Spacing and blocking

- One blank line between top-level declarations and between logical groups inside a function.
- No blank line padding the inside of a tight block. Never stack multiple blank lines.
- Put a blank line before each multi-line closure or block so it reads as its own unit.

### Comments on declarations

- Do not stack a comment on top of each consecutive `let`, `const`, or interface field.
- Consolidate the rationale into one comment above the group, or move it to the closure or function that acts on the state.
- Keep declaration groups comment-free where a name already explains them.

### Imports

- Order is value groups then a type-import block, blank-line separated. The ianvs plugin owns it. Do not reorder by hand.
- Prefer separate `import type` statements over inline `type` specifiers.
- Within a value import, uppercase-initial names (SCREAMING_SNAKE constants and enum values) sort ahead of camelCase functions, so ALL_CAPS names cluster first and never break up a function list.

### Interfaces and classes

- Required fields first, then optional, grouped by concern. Keep member order consistent within a file.
- Keep fields comment-free. Put any explanation in the interface's top JSDoc, or name the field so it needs none.
- In a class, declare an index signature and fields before methods, and public members before private ones. Author the order by hand, because `member-ordering` reports these as warnings with no autofix.
- Required-before-optional spans the whole class, methods included. An optional instance field placed before a method trips the rule, so declare it required with an explicit `undefined` such as `allowedNodeIds: Set<string> | undefined = undefined`.

### Examples

Guard wraps, so it takes braces:

```typescript
const resolved = resolveCatalogId(params.catalogId)
if (!resolved.id) {
  return textResult(resolved.message ?? "Unknown catalog id.")
}
const catalogId = resolved.id
```

A long body wraps under Prettier, so brace it from the start. Never author a bare one-line guard whose body is a long template literal:

```typescript
// Wrong: Prettier wraps the body onto the next line, which fails curly: multi-line.
if (result.isValid)
  return `Valid: ${resolved.id} may be inserted under ${String(params.parentId)}.`

// Right: braces from the start.
if (result.isValid) {
  return `Valid: ${resolved.id} may be inserted under ${String(params.parentId)}.`
}
```

Value imports first, then a type block; constants ahead of functions:

```typescript
import { ORDERED_COMPONENT_LEVELS, getComponentSchema, isComponentId } from "@seldon/core"

import type { Board, ComponentLevel, Workspace } from "@seldon/core"
```

Named parameter type, grouped return fields, no comments on fields:

```typescript
interface BuildOllamaModelOptions {
  model?: string
  host?: string
  reasoning?: boolean
}
```
